# Line endings.
#
# Development happens on Windows; production is a Linux VPS. Without this, blobs
# went into the repo with mixed endings, so every `git pull` on the server found
# eight files "modified" that nobody had touched and refused to fast-forward —
# a manual `git checkout -- .` before every single deploy.
#
# `text=auto` normalises everything git detects as text to LF *in the repo*.
# `eol=lf` also checks it out as LF on every platform, overriding the local
# core.autocrlf=true, so Windows and the VPS hold byte-identical files and the
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings stop.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts that must keep CRLF to run correctly.
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# Binaries: never touched, never diffed as text. `text=auto` detects most of
# these on its own, but a misdetection corrupts the file, and being explicit
# costs nothing.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.mp3  binary
*.ogg  binary
*.wav  binary
*.webm binary
*.mp4  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.pdf  binary
